==================================================
Lab 9: SQL Injection Attack - Listing the Database
Contents on Non-Oracle Databases
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

Up until now, whenever we wanted to steal a username and password, we already knew exactly which table and
column names to use, because the lab walkthroughs simply told us the table was called users and the columns
were called username and password. In a real attack, nobody hands you this information directly. This lab
teaches how to discover the actual table and column names yourself, starting from nothing, using only the
database’s own built-in list of everything it contains.

Goal: discover the real name of the table holding user credentials, discover which columns in it hold the
username and password, and then use that information to steal every user’s login details.

==============================
WHAT IS INFORMATION_SCHEMA

Almost every major database, except Oracle, which has its own different system for this, comes with a
special built-in section called information_schema. This is not something application developers created, it
exists automatically as part of the database software itself, and its entire purpose is to describe the
database’s own structure. It contains tables that list other tables, and tables that list the columns inside
those tables. In other words, it is a kind of built-in map of the whole database, and critically, we can query
it using completely normal SELECT statements, the exact same way we would query any other table.

=============================
STEP 1: CONFIRM COLUMN COUNT AND TEXT COMPATIBILITY

As always before starting any UNION attack, we confirm the query returns two columns, and that both accept
text, using two simple placeholder text values. This step is identical to earlier UNION labs, and is just there
to make sure our later, more meaningful payloads will actually fit correctly into the existing query structure.

=============================
STEP 2: LIST ALL TABLE NAMES

Instead of querying some normal business-related table, we query information_schema.tables, a special table
that exists purely to list the name of every single table that currently exists in the entire database. This
query has absolutely nothing to do with the actual website or its products, it is only describing the
database’s own internal structure.

Running this through our UNION attack causes every table name in the whole database to show up, one at a time,
in place of where a product name would normally appear. Scrolling through this long list, we look specifically
for a table name that sounds like it might hold user accounts, such as one starting with the word users,
usually followed by some random extra characters added by the lab to make it harder to guess in advance.

=============================
STEP 3: LIST THE COLUMNS INSIDE THAT TABLE

Once we have found the real table name, we need to know what columns exist inside it, since we cannot guess
whether it uses column names like username and password, or something slightly different.

To find this out, we query a second special table, information_schema.columns, which lists every column that
exists inside every table in the database. By adding a condition that filters this list down to only the
specific table name we just found, we get back only the column names belonging to that one table, instead of
being overwhelmed with every column from every table in the whole database.

Scrolling through this shorter, filtered list, we look for column names that sound like they would hold a
username and a password, again possibly with some random extra characters attached to their names.

=============================
STEP 4: RETRIEVE THE ACTUAL DATA

Now that we know the real table name and the real column names, we can finally query the actual data we were
after from the very beginning. This step is no different in spirit from the very first UNION data-theft lab, we
are simply selecting two real column names from a real table name, except this time, we had to work to discover
what those real names actually were, instead of being told them directly.

The result is the same as before, every username paired with its corresponding password, displayed directly on
the page, with the administrator’s row somewhere among them.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and intercept the category filter request using Burp Suite.
Confirm two text-compatible columns using a simple UNION SELECT with two placeholder values.
Query information_schema.tables to list every table name in the database, and identify the one likely to hold
user credentials.
Query information_schema.columns, filtered to that specific table name, to list its column names, and identify
the ones likely to hold a username and a password.
Query the real table using the real column names you just discovered, to retrieve every username and password
pair.
Find the administrator’s row in the results, and use that password to log in manually.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

Since the real table and column names are randomly generated for each individual lab instance, there is no way
to know them in advance or guess them automatically. Because of this, the task is broken into three small,
separate steps, run one at a time, rather than one single automatic script, since a human needs to actually read
the output at each stage before the next step can be built correctly.

The first small script sends a UNION SELECT querying information_schema.tables, asking for every table name
that exists in the database. It cleans up the HTML response the same way as earlier labs, stripping tags and
blank lines, then prints every remaining line so the real table name can be spotted by scanning through the
output.

The second small script asks for the table name just discovered, and sends a UNION SELECT querying
information_schema.columns, filtered down to only that one table using a WHERE condition. It prints the cleaned
results the same way, so the real username and password column names can be identified from the shorter,
filtered list.

The third and final small script asks for the table name and both column names discovered so far, builds a
UNION SELECT using those exact real names, and sends it. The cleaned response is printed, showing every
username and password pair stored in that table, including the administrator’s.

=============================
WHY THIS IS SPLIT INTO SEPARATE STEPS INSTEAD OF ONE SCRIPT

Earlier labs could be fully automated end to end because every detail needed, such as the table name users or
the column names username and password, was already known in advance from the lab’s own description. Here, the
real names only exist inside this one specific lab instance, randomly generated, and the only way to learn them
is to actually look at what the database itself reports back. A script cannot guess a random name it was never
told, so a human has to read the first query’s results, decide which name looks correct, and manually carry that
real name forward into the next query.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The underlying flaw is the same root cause as every previous lab, user input reaches a SQL query without being
checked or cleaned. What makes this lab different is realizing that a UNION attack does not have to be limited
to querying the application’s own normal tables, it can just as easily query the database’s own internal
description of itself, which exists specifically so that administrators and tools can inspect the database’s
structure, but which an attacker can abuse the exact same way.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries so user input can never change the structure of a SQL query, regardless of whether
the attacker is targeting application data or the database’s own internal structure information.

Other good practices:

Restrict database account permissions so the account used by the web application has no reason to read from
information_schema at all, since a normal application almost never needs to look up its own table structure
during regular operation.
Avoid displaying raw, unfiltered query results directly on public pages, since this is exactly what allows an
attacker to read back anything a UNION attack manages to pull out, whether it is real data or structural
information about the database itself.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

Almost every database other than Oracle includes a built-in information_schema, which can be queried using
completely normal SELECT statements to discover every table and column that exists.
Real-world table and column names are rarely as obvious as users, username, and password, and attackers often
need to discover these names themselves before they can steal anything meaningful.
This discovery process usually happens in stages, first finding table names, then narrowing down to column
names within a specific table, and only then actually retrieving the real data.
Even though this takes more steps than labs where everything is already known, the fundamental technique is
still just a UNION attack, applied to the database’s own self-describing structure instead of its normal
application data.
